The spec below computes the required answer by brute force. It checks every contiguous window `xs[i..j]`, keeps the windows whose elements all equal `xs[i]`, and takes the longest one. This works differently from the scan-with-reset approach in the code, so the two are unlikely to share a mistake. Every loop is bounded by `len(xs)`, which is at most 7, so all loops fit within the verifier's unroll limit of 8.

```python
def pre(xs):
    if len(xs) > 7:
        return False
    for x in xs:
        if x < 0 or x > 3:
            return False
    return True


def _longest_run(xs):
    n = len(xs)
    best = 0
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(i, n):
            # check whether xs[i..j] are all equal
            ok = True
            for k in range(i, j + 1):
                if xs[k] != xs[i]:
                    ok = False
            if ok and (j - i + 1) > best:
                best = j - i + 1
    return best


def post(xs, result):
    return result == _longest_run(xs)
```
